{"input_text": "Question: So I need to calculate $f'(\sqrt{\pi})+g'(\sqrt{\pi})$ of the integrals $f(x)=(\int_{0}^{x}e^{-t^2}dt)^2$ and $g(x)=\int_{0}^{1}\frac{e^{-x^2(1+t^2)}}{1+t^2}dt$ 
 First I used differentiation under the integral sign rule and try to solve it, 
 for the first integral , I got $f'(x)=2 (\int_{0}^{x}e^{-t^2}dt) e^{-x}$, then $f'(\sqrt\pi)=2 (\int_{0}^{\sqrt\pi}e^{-t^2}dt) e^{-\sqrt\pi}$. Now I don't know how to solve the integral from $0$ to $\sqrt\pi$. I am also unable to solve the next integral as well, as same integral occurs after differentiantion. Please help me to solve this. 

Answer: $$f(x)=\left(\int_{0}^{x}e^{-t^{2}}dt\right)^{2} \implies f'(x)=2e^{-x^{2}}\int_{0}^{x}e^{-t^{2}}dt$$ 
 \begin{align*}
g'(x)=\int_{0}^{1}\frac{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left( e^{-x^{2}(1+t^{2})}\right)}{1+t^{2}}dt&=-\int_{0}^{1}2xe^{-x^{2}(1+t^{2})}dt\\
&=-2xe^{-x^2}\int_{0}^{1}e^{-x^2t^2}dt\\
&=-2e^{-x^{2}}\int_{0}^{x}e^{-z^{2}}dz\qquad \text{substituting  $xt=z$}
\end{align*} 
Hence  $f'(x)+g'(x)=0$  for all  $x$ . 
