 Any help is appreciated. 

Answer: This is a consequence of the fact that there are infinitely many irreducible polynomials in the UFD $\Bbb{F}_q[x]$. All the elements of $\Bbb{F}_q[f_1,f_2,\ldots,f_k]$ are of the form $r(x)/s(x)$ with $r(x),s(x)\in\Bbb{F}_q[x]$ such that the irreducible factors of the denominator $s(x)$ are those of the denominators $q_i$
of the generators $f_i$. 
 So if we select an irreducible polynomial $s(x)$ that is not a factor of any of the polynomials $q_i$, then $1/s(x)\notin\Bbb{F}_q[f_1,\ldots,f_k]$. The claim follows. 
